WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:08.170
Malcolm Grant: Good morning ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.500
Welcome to this meeting of the Board of NHS
England.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.570
We are meeting here this morning in London.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:20.350
I am particularly pleased to be able to welcome
a number of members of the public who have

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.350
joined us for this meeting.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:26.710
You are joined, of course, by those who are
able to observe the meeting by a live webcast

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:32.580
that we have introduced as a major way of
doing business in NHS England.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:38.770
We are very clear that we are taking decisions
at these meetings that affect the health of

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.890
the population of England.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:47.670
We have a heavy responsibility to ensure that
the rationale for those decisions and our

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:55.409
ways of working are available and viewable
by those who wish to see these decisions being

00:00:55.409 --> 00:00:56.469
taken.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:02.190
I stress that this is a meeting which we conduct
in public and our exchanges are in the public

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.190
domain.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:10.000
It is not a public meeting in the sense that
it is not a meeting in which we are inviting

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.240
interventions from members of the public.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.579
I stress that is an important constraint upon
the way in which we work because we engage

00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:25.210
in many other ways with public opinion and
with patients and a number of those operations

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.930
will be discussed around the Board table this
morning.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:36.049
I should stress also that this is not the
only way in which the Board works.

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:40.670
At almost every board meeting we will find
some time the day before, in what we call

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:48.060
a development session, in which we argue some
of the big strategic issues affecting the

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:54.590
NHS at a time when they are in an early stage
of gestation so that by the time they have

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.670
been worked though by the executive team and
brought back formally to the meeting of the

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.500
Board, the whole of the Board has been involved
with them.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.790
Yesterday, for example, we spent some time
on a stock take of our first year of progress.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:15.950
This is our first meeting as a second year
fully operation NHS England Board, and we

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.879
also spent some time thinking about the future
strategy for the Board and this will become

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:26.020
crystallised as we go through the coming year.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.819
At the end of the meeting this morning we
will go in to a private session of the Board

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:36.520
to consider some material which is commercially
confidential and which it would be detrimental

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:43.159
to the taxpayer interests for us to have discussions
of in public because it would confer potentially

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:54.659
commercial advantage on those with whom we
are dealing in relation to a sensitive issue.

00:02:54.659 --> 00:03:10.739
I am now going to open the meeting formally.

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:18.590
I report apologies of absence from Sir Bruce
Keogh who is aboard.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:22.770
I welcome particularly this morning two new
members to the Board.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:29.280
Simon Stephens, who has joined us as our new
Chief Executive and who will be in a moment

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.939
presenting his first report to the Board,
and Karen Wheeler, who has joined us on secondment

00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:40.519
from the Department of Health and is our new
Director of Transformations and Corporate

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:41.720
Operations.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.959
Please, our very warmest welcome to both of
you and we are looking forward enormously

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:51.140
to the leadership you will bring to the Board
and all of our work.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:58.110
We also have with us Sir Cyril Chandler who
will join the table when he delivers the report

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.260
of the quality and clinical risk committee.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:07.360
I should also mention that we are in the process
at the moment of recruiting two, or potentially

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.940
three, new non-executive members to the Board.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:18.530
Members will recall that we lost the services
at the end of last year of [inaudible] and

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:27.280
we are trying to find an appropriate candidate
who will bring a similar set of skills and

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.460
expertise to the benefit of the Board.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:35.190
We are in the final processes of having shortlisted
candidates.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:39.960
There will be interviews towards the end of
the month and I hope that the Secretary of

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.539
State will shortly thereafter be in a position
to make formal appointments.

00:04:44.539 --> 00:04:52.180
That will bring the compliment of non-executive
members of the Board up to nine as opposed

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:58.460
to the present six, alongside the non-executive
members, of course, at the members of the

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:03.000
executive team of NHS England

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.720
Let me then launch the business by inviting
any declarations of interest in any matters

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.650
on the agenda, knowing of course that we have
a register of interests in which the standing

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.871
interests are declared, but may I invite any
declaration of interest in any particular

00:05:18.871 --> 00:05:21.600
items on the agenda.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:22.960
No.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:23.960
Thank you very much.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.490
Let me then take then the minutes of the last
meeting.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:34.530
I have had no points raised to me so I am
assuming that you are willing to accept those

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:39.100
minutes as an accurate record of the proceedings
thank you.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.810
Matters arising: I have had no indication
of matters arising other than those that are

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:43.830
already on the agenda.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.479
Can I just confirm that?

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:48.009
Thank you.

00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:50.600
Let me turn to the Chief Executive's report.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.199
Simon, welcome and over to you.

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:53.569
Simon Stevens: Thank you Malcolm.

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:56.880
It is a great pleasure to be here.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.919
First of all I want to say thank you very
much to the patients and the staff and the

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:05.550
carers who have taken the time to meet with
me around the country on the visits I have

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.400
been on over the last several weeks.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.509
This has given me a chance to share their
views and hear about experiences.

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:17.230
It also reminded me that we have a lot to
be proud of as a nation about the National

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.550
Health Service, as well as recognition that
there are some big challenges in front of

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.460
us and we have got a lot of work to do.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:28.569
In my Chief Executive's report this morning
I have picked out some of the items where

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:34.030
we are focussed over the next several weeks
and months.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:39.889
I am going to pick out a few of those and
we will ask one or two colleagues to come

00:06:39.889 --> 00:06:42.900
in on some of those specific points.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:48.449
In terms of commissioning performance I think
we know that one of the big gains of the last

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:55.500
decade or so has been the fact that the NHS
has made it much quicker and safer for people

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.000
who need routine surgery to have their operations.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:05.819
If you think back ten years people were routinely
waiting a year and a half for an operation

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:12.180
and now the vast majority of people are being
seen, not in 18 months, but in 18 weeks from

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.590
the point of referral to treatment.

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:18.410
Equally we know that there are substantial
pressures around that.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:26.270
It is very important, I think, that as CCGs
and hospitals and other providers refine their

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:32.509
plans now for 2014 and 2015 we use the next
several weeks to ensure that there is appropriate

00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:37.879
focus on maintaining that position through
the year, recognising that that is going to

00:07:37.879 --> 00:07:44.280
require some renewed focus over the next several
months to get us in the spring and the summer

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.569
to where we want to be.

00:07:46.569 --> 00:07:52.569
Waiting times performance is an area I know
CCGs alongside NHS England and respective

00:07:52.569 --> 00:07:56.680
specialised services are going to want to
put particular focus.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.560
That will be one thing.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.120
The second thing I would comment on would
be the Better Care Fund.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:09.879
This obviously is an opportunity to kick start
a new set of conversations in out-of-hospital

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:16.680
care where there is wide agreement that more
concerted action can be taken at the health

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.590
and social care interface.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.789
The Better Care Fund, together with various
other programmes, including the new proactive

00:08:23.789 --> 00:08:29.800
primary care, the new GMS contract, is part
of the effort to do that.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:34.030
But to the extent that the 1.9 billion that
is going to be invested in the Better Care

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:42.440
Fund next year incrementally does not offset
activity elsewhere in the NHS, then that is

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:48.440
going to be a part of the healthcare system
that is under pressure next year.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:55.070
It will still represent, in all likelihood,
a good use of public money but those two thoughts

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:57.930
are not inconsistent with each other.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:03.030
The good news is we still have got 11 months
or so to get the planning on this right.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:06.460
This is being looked at well in advance.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:13.200
The early look at the detailed local plans
shows that there is still quite a bit of work

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.480
needed, frankly, to get the rigour in terms
of quantifying the use of the resources and

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.580
the impact that is going to be had.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:25.411
I think what we are going to be working with
CCGs, with health and wellbeing boards and

00:09:25.411 --> 00:09:30.680
with local government partners on over the
next several weeks are four things.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:35.820
Firstly, making sure that we are really vigorously
kicking the tyres and quantifying the impact

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.760
that is expected to occur on emergency admissions,
not that those will not be the only benefits

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:45.040
that will flow from this but understanding
in practice what that is likely to be and

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:47.710
getting some of the measurement systems in
place to do that.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:54.580
Secondly, we want to ensure that acute hospitals
are fully engaged in that conversation locally.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:10:02.020
Thirdly, we want to ensure that there is proper
financial risk sharing between the bed care

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:07.000
fund and the expected down stream impacts
that are occurring.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:13.010
And fourthly, I am going to be asking CCG
accountable officers to provide an attestation

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:18.770
in due course that the Better Care Fund investments
represent an appropriate use of public money.

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.850
I am sure they will.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:27.480
I am looking for the NHS to be playing a full
and constructive part in this conversation,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:30.430
not to be commenting from the sidelines.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:36.170
Equally I want to have a very frank conversation
locally about what will and will not be achieved

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:40.440
and how that is going to be done so that we
are having that conversation now rather than

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:45.340
chickens coming home to roost in January,
February, March, if that does not occur.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.750
So that is the Better Care Fund.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:54.320
On CCG co-commissioning of primary care; I
think this has been well covered.

00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:00.120
The thought that Roz and others with the CCGs
had been thinking about for some time now,

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.430
along with the commissioning assembly and
the CCG (the clinical commissioners group),

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:10.790
making the point to us is that there is great
value in finding ways of a unified, population

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:15.060
based approach to the judgements that are
made about primary care spending, mental health

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:20.380
spending, as well as the local hospital spending
and the specialised commissioning spending.

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:25.970
This is one way of helping respond to that;
recognising that CCGs are in a different place,

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:32.870
and so we want to hear their ideas in a permissive
way, while having a very clear duty to ensure

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:38.260
that there are full safe guards around conflicts
of interest and we as an executive will not

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:45.510
be recommending to the Board of NHS England
sign off of any form of co-commissioning unless

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:51.840
we can all be confident that that test has
been met.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:57.900
In my note, it elaborates a little on the
comments I made to the Health Select committee

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:03.460
about the importance of the NHS establishing
a forward view as to what the next five years

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:07.830
might look like, and what the next steps we
need to take on the back of the call to action

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:11.790
that NHS England launched last year.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:19.640
I will aim to say more about that in my speech
to the NHS confederation in early June.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:23.510
In terms of NHS England as an organisation
I think there is widespread recognition based

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:29.560
on the response from the stakeholder survey,
the conversations with CCGs and what our own

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:35.330
staff say that actually it has been a huge
success in its first year of operation to

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:40.570
be able to make the transition from the many
constituent organisations that came together

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:45.980
to form NHS England; huge amount of work,
a lot of progress has been made in the first

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:46.980
year.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:52.640
I can say that, even if you can not, as somebody
coming in and seeing it and being very impressed

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:55.970
by so much of what has been done.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:13:00.270
Equally I think there is recognition that
this is a great moment to take a breath and

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:05.880
look at how we now move to the next phase
of the work that we want to do.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:10.930
In terms of our own organisation I think what
we are doing really is three things.

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:17.690
One, we are making sure that we have got a
really tight focus on the things the commissioning

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:22.100
body NHS England, through the mandate, is
here to deliver.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:29.190
Two, ensuring that in terms of our internal
processes, directorial structures and relationships

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:33.260
between the national and local part of the
NHS, we have got proper alignment through

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:34.386
the director of structures relationships and
between the local and national part of the

00:13:34.386 --> 00:13:35.386
NHS.

00:13:35.386 --> 00:13:37.800
Thirdly, making sure we have got the right
competencies and capabilities specifically

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:41.880
focussed on the commissioning responsibilities
we have got.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:47.610
Focus, alignment, competencies and capabilities;
those are the three things we are getting

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.050
to grips with as part of this process.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:57.640
The specific take home from that is that with
Bill's departure I will be proceeding to fill

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:02.950
the role of Commissioning Strategy Director,
and as we will come on and talk about the

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:09.580
context of specialised commissioning, I will
also be filling the role of Director of Specialised

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:10.580
Commissioning.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:13.610
So those are two of the immediate next steps
but there is a broader process of work under

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:19.380
way that Karen and Roz are leading on behalf
of the executive group around some of the

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:24.820
other changes we will make incrementally across
the organisation.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:32.000
I think the last thing I would say, just to
underline my report here, would focus on the

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:38.170
great gains there have been through the approach
to transparency that this organisation has

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:39.170
taken.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.850
Frankly in its first year of operation there
has been a huge departure from what went before

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:47.610
with many of the decisions that you have been
taken, whether it be allocations or other

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:53.990
approaches made in public rather than as a
government department, as it were.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:59.640
That democratic accountability through the
mandate, the publication of material – I

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.940
think we are now going to build on that with
one further change.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:11.021
We will start on a quarterly basis publishing
information on the meetings that we as the

00:15:11.021 --> 00:15:15.470
executive directors, I as Chief Executive,
have with non-NHS bodies in line with the

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:23.130
approach taken by government departments.

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:31.260
I think when we do that, it will show we are
a listening organisation that makes no apologies

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:36.660
at all for being out and about talking to
many different people and that will be there

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.110
for all to see.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.170
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:48.020
May I invite any questions or comments from
member of the Board?

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:50.029
Moira Gibb: Thank you Simon, very helpful.

00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:58.860
To link better care fund and mental care services
are you anticipating that 

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:06.300
the parity of esteem will be applying in the
better care fund?

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:12.200
Secondly given that the parity of esteem is
a powerful ambition how does it work for children's

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:13.670
mental health?

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:23.450
Simon Stevens: On the Better Care Fund the
opportunity is there for health and wellbeing

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:30.510
boards to invest in support for carers, social
care, primary care, mental health services,

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:32.131
acute services where that makes sense.

00:16:32.131 --> 00:16:35.290
That is not something we have pre-judged.

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:40.770
What we do want is, however, great clarity
about what those investments are and what

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:46.410
they are intended to do, and then some mechanism
for ensuring that they do that or if they

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:49.830
do not when risk arises that is covered off
through the fund.

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:54.570
More generally there are a number of things
that we are now doing to advance the parity

00:16:54.570 --> 00:17:00.680
of esteem agenda for mental health and I would
just reference a couple of them.

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:07.970
One is that until this April there was not
parity of esteem in terms of the choices available

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:11.530
to people being referred for elected mental
health services.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:17.890
You had a legal right if you were using physical
health services to make a choice of the provider

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:22.510
you were being referred to; you did not have
that legal right if you going to be receiving

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:23.970
mental health services.

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:29.270
Fortunately that was changed from April and
we are about to go out to a 12 week consultation

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:32.350
on what that will look like and how that will
work.

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:36.210
So that will be an important development,
I hope.

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:42.030
Secondly, we know there are 300,000 people
across England with severe mental health problems

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:47.980
who are dying up to 15 years younger than
the rest of the population, partly as a result

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:50.870
of physical health problems, not mental health
problems.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:57.800
So NHS England has just launched – Geraldine
Strathdee, National Director of Mental Health

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:03.900
would I think say – the world's biggest
test of the idea that by changing the payment

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:08.990
incentives we can actually get people to improve
the physical health of people with severe

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:09.990
mental health problems.

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:17.520
So a sequin scheme, as it were, is going live
which will actually fund efforts to improve

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:22.350
the physical health of many people with severe
mental health problems whose life expectancy,

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:27.730
on average, is about the same as the population
of England in the 1950s.

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:32.170
Parity of esteem without tackling that is
just hot air so it will be very important

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.290
that that makes significant progress.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:41.010
On children's and adolescent mental health
services, the impression I have got from talking

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:45.900
with mental heath services around the country,
maybe Victor and others have seen this as

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.480
well, is that they under severe pressure.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:51.540
I think this is an area we are going to have
to take a very close look at.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:52.540
I do not know, Jane, whether you have been
involved in that

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:53.540
Jane Cummings: More recently, but absolutely
right.

00:18:53.540 --> 00:19:02.530
The amount of pressure particularly of CAM
services is significant at the moment.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:05.820
I think it is a priority for us to address.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:10.120
Victor Adebowale: First of all I welcome the
report.

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:16.160
I think there are a number of things in it
that are kind of firsts actually.

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:19.130
First of all I really welcome the attention
you have paid to mental health.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:24.850
I have chaired the parity of esteem board
and I would certainly agree with the comments

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:28.290
that you have just made.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:33.010
There is a strand of the work of the parity
of esteem board which refers specifically

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:34.010
to children and young people.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:38.570
It is my view that we need a root and branch
review of what CAM is about.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:42.360
My personal view is that it is not fit for
purpose.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:46.510
There needs to be a relationship between CAM,
mental health and primary care that makes

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:48.120
sense.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.890
You did not say anything about crisis response.

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:54.920
I agree with everything you say but I would
add that you do not have parity of esteem

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:58.810
unless you have parity of response when you
are in crisis and that is a priority for the

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:03.980
work of the Board and you might want to comment
further on that.

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:09.501
My other comment was again a very welcome
response to your reference to the Roger Kline

00:20:09.501 --> 00:20:12.230
report and the Snowy White peaks.

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:18.990
I note that it is really good to have a chief
executive who comes in to the NHS with a concern

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:25.270
for equality as opposed to what usually happens.

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:30.370
You reference a conversation with Leadership
Academy, and I would not mind you saying a

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:34.370
bit more about that because Roger Kline's
report is not actually just about race, it

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:38.310
is about the impact of race on patient care,
and the leadership.

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:40.650
There are three things going on here.

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:47.490
There's a race issue, which actually has an
impact on patient care and the leadership

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:52.940
academy's role in ensuring that the leadership
of the NHS understands that impact.

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:56.650
I just wondered whether you would say more
about that and whether you are going to be

00:20:56.650 --> 00:20:59.600
saying more about it, as it is a big challenge
for us.

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:00.600
Simon Stevens: Yes I am.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:05.840
I must admit I was very struck by Roger's
report which appeared to show that in terms

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:13.000
of governance of trusts in London, which is
probably not unrepresentative, frankly we

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:14.000
have gone backwards.

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:17.190
It is not even that we have not made the progress
we should have done.

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:23.780
It is pretty obvious that, as the data shows,
if the people running organisations are not

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:28.760
connected to the communities they are serving
then that has a clear impact on the accessibility

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:29.910
on inequalities.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:37.040
We actually see that some of the most dissatisfied
people with our services are from BMA communities

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:40.520
across London and other cities.

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:45.880
I think of some of the visits I have been
doing in Birmingham, for example recently,

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:48.710
which actually links to your crisis points
in mental health services.

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:55.270
There is a disproportionate use of some of
those services, because of inadequate access

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:59.690
to upstream mental health and physical health
services for some of those communities.

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:01.550
There is a huge agenda here.

00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:07.550
The NHS equalities and diversity council,
which I have inherited the chairing of and

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:12.700
which I am more than happy to do, I have asked
them by the time of our next meeting which

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:18.990
is 29th July to actually come back with a
concrete set of actions that they think we

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:25.740
should all be driving so we can actually see
some proper progress on this agenda.

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:34.220
Ciarán Devane: Two things, one is I completely
agree with what you say.

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:38.600
It is amazing we have got as far as we have
without a car crash or whatever the right

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:39.600
analogy is.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:40.610
Simon Stevens: You are paraphrasing, now.

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:42.570
Ciarán Devane: Yes, I am.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:45.110
But it is, and there has been a huge amount
of work.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:47.170
I think there is also more to go.

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:51.910
I think they are making it easy for staff
to do their jobs.

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:58.580
I think it is important we make sure we deliver
on some of the contract.

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:02.730
We do things when we say we are going to do
things for people.

00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:04.430
That was one comment.

00:23:04.430 --> 00:23:05.930
Second one though was around co-commissioning.

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:12.260
Yes there is a conflict of interest thing
and I am sure we can sort that, but how does

00:23:12.260 --> 00:23:16.490
this sit with the role of a health and wellbeing
board because actually if we want to do population

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:19.250
based commissioning we need social care in
there.

00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:25.810
It is not just the NHS mechanisms of CCGs
and local area teams talking to each other,

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:31.470
there are social care providers, local authorities,
voluntary sector etc.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:36.250
Is there a risk that actually we are moving
in the right direction but only partially

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:38.630
and missing some of the bigger opportunities?

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:41.680
Simon Stevens: I think there are various points
to make there.

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:46.010
On the conflict of interest point, of course
health and wellbeing boards have providers

00:23:46.010 --> 00:23:47.150
represented as well.

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:52.710
They have exactly the same kind of governance
issues that they need to resolve as does a

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:55.480
CCG or working with NHS England.

00:23:55.480 --> 00:24:02.309
I think your underlying point is an important
one which is that health and wellbeing boards

00:24:02.309 --> 00:24:06.570
in principle offer the opportunity to take
a broad look, not just at health spending,

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:08.400
but at social care spending.

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:14.330
The good news is that it appears that in many
parts of the country they are using the opportunity

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:23.280
to pool more than the minimum required in
the better care funds so people are very excited

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:25.740
by the possibilities that this represents.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:33.870
That said, I think how the Better Care Fund,
specifically, is planned and executed will

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:40.290
be a real test of the robustness of health
and wellbeing boards as a platform for this

00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:41.440
kind of approach.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:47.710
So it will be very important that they work
effectively in doing this, if more is to be

00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:49.640
placed on to them in future.

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:51.260
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:55.150
Can I just then raise with you one of the
issues that you have discussed in the report,

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:58.550
that of transparency.

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:05.640
I think we all welcome very much the commitment
to ensuring that meetings of the executive

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:11.840
team with outside bodies are recorded and
reported commonly with other government bodies,

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:17.330
but the issue alongside that I think is the
question of expenses which has been in the

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:18.330
media recently.

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:24.070
I think the issue here which troubles me a
great deal is to ensure there is a proper

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:27.500
understanding of the way in which NHS England
works.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:32.450
There is a responsibility of every member
of the executive team not to sit in a central

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:38.470
office and commute from home everyday but
to be out and around the country, engaging

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:43.220
with our patients, engaging with the providers,
with the CCGs and in the case particularly

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:47.710
of the medical director and the nursing director
with their own profession around the country.

00:25:47.710 --> 00:25:54.810
I fully understand this involves people being
away from home night after night, being engaged

00:25:54.810 --> 00:26:00.380
in activities in every part of England and
that is actually a critical part of what we

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:01.380
expect of them.

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:08.180
What I do not want to occur is a sense that
publicity about the sheer cost of doing this,

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:13.680
the reasonable cost conducted with appropriate
thrift, should in any way inhibit people from

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.590
doing their jobs properly.

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:24.180
I would like, Simon, if you could help us
to assure our teams across NHS England, that

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:27.140
this is still our expectation of how they
would behave.

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:28.140
Simon Stevens: Exactly.

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:32.750
One of the great things about this organisation
is that in its first year of operation it

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:37.680
has actually been incredibly engaged, out
and about around the country with the communities

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:38.680
that we serve.

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:39.840
That is not my assessment.

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:45.950
That is the assessment of the national stakeholders,
who have been independently asked about this.

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:49.620
We are posting the survey response on our
website today.

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:51.930
People can see what people are saying.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:57.370
They like the fact we are transparent and
out and about; so it is absolutely vital that

00:26:57.370 --> 00:26:59.430
we continue to do that.

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:05.960
And to conflate issues around expenses, with
issues around the need for travel is a complete

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:09.700
non sequitur, which we must ensure does not
inhibit what we are doing.

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:10.700
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:14.000
Are there any more issues or queries on Simon's
report?

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:15.000
No?

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:15.050
Thank you very much

